              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "Opioids can be derived from natural, semi-synthetic, or entirely synthetic sources. Their origin determines how they are classified:\n\n### 1. Natural Opioids (Opiates)\nNatural opioids\u2014traditionally referred to as **opiates**\u2014are derived directly from the **opium poppy plant (*Papaver somniferum*)**. \n* **How they are sourced:** When the seed pod of the opium poppy is scraped, it secretes a milky, sticky sap (latex). This raw opium is harvested and refined to extract natural active chemical compounds.\n* **Examples:** \n  * **Morphine** (the primary active ingredient in opium)\n  * **Codeine**\n  * **Thebaine** (a minor alkaloid used primarily to synthesize other drugs)\n\n### 2. Semi-Synthetic Opioids\nSemi-synthetic opioids are created in a laboratory by chemically altering the natural alkaloids found in the opium poppy. Scientists use natural opiates as a starting block and modify their molecular structure to make them stronger, longer-lasting, or more easily absorbed by the body.\n* **Examples:**\n  * **Heroin** (diacetylmorphine, made by chemically modifying morphine)\n  * **Oxycodone** (OxyContin, made from thebaine)\n  * **Hydrocodone** (Vicodin)\n  * **Hydromorphone** (Dilaudid)\n\n### 3. Synthetic Opioids\nSynthetic opioids are entirely **man-made in chemical laboratories**. They do not contain any natural poppy plant matter. Instead, chemists design these artificial compounds from scratch to mimic the molecular structure of natural opiates, allowing them to bind to the exact same opioid receptors in the human brain and body.\n* **Examples:**\n  * **Fentanyl** (a highly potent painkiller)\n  * **Methadone** (often used in addiction treatment)\n  * **Tramadol**\n\n### Why do they work?\nWhether natural, semi-synthetic, or synthetic, all opioids work because their chemical structures resemble the body's naturally occurring endorphins. They bind to opioid receptors in the brain, spinal cord, and gastrointestinal tract, effectively blocking pain signals and producing a feelings of pain relief and, in some cases, euphoria.",
